pub fn title -> String = "Grammar reference"
pub fn summary -> String =
  "Seseragi's syntax is written here using EBNF, a notation for grammars. The articles for each construct explain how that syntax behaves."
pub fn readingTitle -> String = "How to read this grammar"
pub fn reading -> String =
  "A grammar describes which sequences of symbols can be written in source. EBNF below is notation for that description, not a program to execute. Start with a short rule containing two alternatives."
pub fn lexical -> String =
  "INTEGER and LOWER_IDENTIFIER label classified pieces of source; they do not tell you to copy those words into a program. INTEGER is an integer literal. LOWER_IDENTIFIER includes names beginning with lowercase letters and names such as 次の値 whose initial character has no case. Identifier characters follow the Unicode XID rules; names beginning with uppercase characters are UPPER_IDENTIFIER. NEWLINE and source semicolons separate declarations or block items. Indentation depth does not delimit a block."
pub fn grammarTitle -> String = "Complete grammar"
pub fn grammarBody -> String =
  "This grammar is read from the language specification during the site build. It brings together the syntax for modules, declarations, expressions, patterns, and types."
pub fn limitsTitle -> String = "Valid syntax can still have a type error"
pub fn limits -> String =
  "Code can match the grammar and still fail because it uses an undefined name or passes a value of the wrong type. Function application associates to the left; function types associate to the right. The precedence and associativity assigned to each operator determine how an expression groups. Conditions such as covering every case in match or making a declaration public cannot be determined from the syntax alone. Read the corresponding article for its meaning and restrictions."
pub fn ambiguity -> String =
  "For example, {} is an empty record, while { expression } is a block. A single-field shorthand record needs a comma, as in { x, }. Parentheses group an expression, form a tuple, or denote Unit. To pass multiple function arguments, separate them with spaces, as in f x y."
pub fn relatedTitle -> String = "Read the rules behind the productions"
pub fn sourceTitle -> String = "Normative source"
pub fn source -> String =
  "These productions come from the language specification's grammar. Rules in the specification also determine conditions that syntax alone does not settle, including name resolution, type checking, and exhaustive matching."

pub fn booleanReading -> String =
  "boolean names the rule. The quoted True and False are the actual words to write in source. | chooses between alternatives, so this rule describes either True or False. Neither boolean = nor the final semicolon belongs to the boolean value written in your program."

pub fn notation -> String =
  "An unquoted name refers to another rule, and commas separate parts written in order. [ ... ] encloses an optional part; { ... } permits zero or more repetitions. These symbols belong to EBNF notation. When the source itself requires brackets or braces, the grammar quotes them."

pub fn exampleTitle -> String = "An actual program rather than grammar notation"

pub fn invalidTitle -> String = "A grammatical form with incompatible value types"

pub fn invalidReading -> String =
  "This let has the grammatical shape name, annotation, =, expression. But it supplies the String \"three\" to count annotated Int, so typechecking reports SES-T0101. Change \"three\" to the integer 3 to print 3. Matching the grammar alone does not establish that names and types satisfy their rules."

pub fn exampleReading -> Array<String> = [
  "In let count: Int = 3, let and = are a keyword and symbol written in source. count names a value and occupies the pattern position in the full let-decl production. : Int is an annotation and 3 is the right-hand expression.",
  "let next = count + 1 omits the annotation. The [ \":\", type ] part of let-decl is optional, so both declarations match the grammar. The newline ending each declaration is its terminator; you do not need to add a semicolon to both.",
  "pub effect fn main is the public entry returning output work to execute. println writes a value and a newline. ${count} and ${next} insert the values inside backticks, so execution displays 3, 4. Reading the grammar and checking these type/evaluation rules are separate tasks.",
]

pub fn relatedPurposes -> Array<String> = [
  "Read concrete examples of Unicode names, comments and character categories.",
  "Check how space-separated arguments in f x y are grouped.",
  "Look up each operator’s precedence and left/right grouping.",
  "Check brace-delimited blocks and the declarations allowed inside.",
  "Read patterns that distinguish value shapes and the requirement to handle every case.",
  "Locate type names and arguments through examples of concrete values.",
  "Check which declarations belong at the file’s top level and how they relate to execution.",
]
